/* NCERT Physics Class XI — Chapter 9: Mechanical Properties of Fluids (book page 180–201)
   type 'num' = Numerical (step-by-step solution in sol); img = inline SVG graph/diagram */
addChapter({
  id: 'phy-11-09',
  subj: 'phy',
  cls: 11,
  no: 9,
  title: 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids',
  src: 'NCERT Physics XI · Chapter 9',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: '9.1 Introduction', t: 'Liquids and gases can flow and are called fluids; this property distinguishes them from solids. Earth has an envelope of air and two-thirds of its surface is covered with water; every mammalian body is mostly water, and processes in living beings including plants are mediated by fluids.' },
    { id: 'in2', topic: 'Fluids vs solids', t: 'A fluid has no definite shape of its own. Solids and liquids have a fixed volume (under atmospheric pressure), whereas a gas fills its container. Solids and liquids have much lower compressibility than gases. The key property of fluids is that they offer very little resistance to shear stress; the shearing stress of fluids is about a million times smaller than that of solids.' },
    { id: 'pr1', topic: '9.2 Pressure', t: 'A sharp needle pierces skin but a blunt object (back of a spoon) with the same force does not; an elephant stepping on a man\'s chest would crack his ribs, but a circus performer is saved by a large, light, strong wooden plank. The smaller the area on which a force acts, the greater the impact — this impact is pressure.' },
    { id: 'pr2', topic: 'Force normal to surface', t: 'A fluid at rest exerts a force on a submerged object that is always normal to its surface; a parallel component would make the fluid flow parallel to the surface (Newton\'s third law), which cannot happen at rest (Fig 9.1a). An idealised pressure-measuring device is an evacuated chamber with a spring-loaded piston (Fig 9.1b).' },
    { id: 'pr3', topic: 'Definition and units', t: 'Average pressure P_av = F/A (9.1); P = lim (ΔA→0) ΔF/ΔA (9.2). Pressure is a scalar (the force in the numerator is the normal component). Dimensions [ML⁻¹T⁻²]; SI unit N m⁻², named pascal (Pa) after French scientist Blaise Pascal (1623–1662). Atmosphere: 1 atm = 1.013 × 10⁵ Pa (pressure at sea level).' },
    { id: 'pr4', topic: 'Density', t: 'Density ρ = m/V (9.3), dimensions [ML⁻³], SI unit kg m⁻³, a positive scalar. A liquid is largely incompressible, so its density is nearly constant; gases show large variation with pressure. Density of water at 4 °C (277 K) is 1.0 × 10³ kg m⁻³. Relative density = density/density of water at 4 °C (dimensionless): aluminium 2.7, density 2.7 × 10³ kg m⁻³.' },
    { id: 't91', topic: 'Table 9.1', t: 'Table 9.1 densities at STP (0 °C, 1 atm), kg m⁻³: water 1.00 × 10³; sea water 1.03 × 10³; mercury 13.6 × 10³; ethyl alcohol 0.806 × 10³; whole blood 1.06 × 10³; air 1.29; oxygen 1.43; hydrogen 9.0 × 10⁻²; interstellar space ≈ 10⁻²⁰.' },
    { id: 'e91', topic: 'Example 9.1', t: 'Example 9.1: two femurs, each of cross-section 10 cm², support the upper body of mass 40 kg. A = 20 × 10⁻⁴ m², F = 400 N (g = 10 m s⁻²); P_av = F/A = 2 × 10⁵ N m⁻².' },
    { id: 'pl1', topic: '9.2.1 Pascal\'s law', t: 'French scientist Blaise Pascal observed that the pressure in a fluid at rest is the same at all points at the same height. For the prism element ABC-DEF (Fig 9.2): F_b sin θ = F_c, F_b cos θ = F_a; A_b sin θ = A_c, A_b cos θ = A_a; so F_b/A_b = F_c/A_c = F_a/A_a, P_b = P_c = P_a (9.4). Pressure is the same in all directions in a fluid at rest; no direction can be assigned to it.' },
    { id: 'pl2', topic: 'Horizontal plane', t: 'For a horizontal bar of fluid in equilibrium, the horizontal forces at the two ends must balance, so pressure is the same at all points in a horizontal plane; otherwise there would be a flow.' },
    { id: 'pd1', topic: '9.2.2 Variation with depth', t: 'For a cylindrical fluid element of base A and height h (Fig 9.3): (P₂ − P₁)A = mg (9.5), with m = ρhA, so P₂ − P₁ = ρgh (9.6). If the top is open to the atmosphere, P = P_a + ρgh (9.7). The excess P − P_a at depth h is the gauge pressure.' },
    { id: 'pd2', topic: 'Hydrostatic paradox', t: 'The area of the cylinder does not appear in the absolute pressure; the height of the fluid column matters, not the base area or shape of the container. Vessels A, B and C of different shapes connected at the bottom (Fig 9.4) fill to the same level though they hold different amounts of water, since water at the bottom has the same pressure — the hydrostatic paradox.' },
    { id: 'e92', topic: 'Example 9.2', t: 'Example 9.2: pressure on a swimmer 10 m below a lake surface: P = P_a + ρgh = 1.01 × 10⁵ + 1000 × 10 × 10 = 2.01 × 10⁵ Pa ≈ 2 atm — a 100% increase. At a depth of 1 km the increase is 100 atm; submarines are designed to withstand such pressures.' },
    { id: 'at1', topic: '9.2.3 Atmospheric pressure', t: 'Atmospheric pressure at a point equals the weight of a column of air of unit cross-section from that point to the top of the atmosphere; at sea level 1.013 × 10⁵ Pa (1 atm). Italian scientist Evangelista Torricelli (1608–1647) devised the mercury barometer (Fig 9.5a): a long tube of mercury inverted into a trough; the space above contains only mercury vapour (pressure negligible), so P_a = ρgh (9.8).' },
    { id: 'at2', topic: 'Units: torr, bar', t: 'The mercury column at sea level is about 76 cm, equivalent to 1 atm. Pressure is often stated in cm or mm of mercury; 1 mm of Hg is 1 torr (after Torricelli): 1 torr = 133 Pa. mm of Hg and torr are used in medicine and physiology; in meteorology the bar and millibar: 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa.' },
    { id: 'at3', topic: 'Open tube manometer', t: 'An open tube manometer is a U-tube with a liquid (low density oil for small pressure differences, mercury for large) — one end open to atmosphere, the other connected to the system (Fig 9.5b). Pressure at A equals pressure at B; it measures gauge pressure P − P_a = ρgh, proportional to manometer height h. Liquids are largely incompressible; gases show large density variations.' },
    { id: 'e93', topic: 'Example 9.3', t: 'Example 9.3: with sea-level air density 1.29 kg m⁻³ constant with altitude, ρgh = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa gives h = 7989 m ≈ 8 km. Actually density and g decrease with height and the atmosphere extends over 100 km. Sea-level pressure is not always 760 mm of Hg; a drop of 10 mm or more is a sign of an approaching storm.' },
    { id: 'e94', topic: 'Example 9.4', t: 'Example 9.4: at depth 1000 m in sea water (ρ = 1.03 × 10³ kg m⁻³, g = 10 m s⁻²): absolute pressure P = 1.01 × 10⁵ + 1.03 × 10⁷ = 104.01 × 10⁵ Pa ≈ 104 atm; gauge pressure ρgh = 103 × 10⁵ Pa ≈ 103 atm. On a 20 cm × 20 cm (0.04 m²) submarine window kept at atmospheric pressure inside, force = 103 × 10⁵ × 0.04 = 4.12 × 10⁵ N.' },
    { id: 'hm1', topic: '9.2.4 Pascal\'s law (transmission)', t: 'In a horizontal cylinder with a piston and three vertical tubes (Fig 9.6a), pushing the piston raises the fluid level equally in all tubes. Whenever external pressure is applied on any part of a fluid contained in a vessel, it is transmitted undiminished and equally in all directions — another form of Pascal\'s law.' },
    { id: 'hm2', topic: 'Hydraulic lift', t: 'In a hydraulic lift (Fig 9.6b), a small piston of area A₁ with force F₁ creates pressure P = F₁/A₁, transmitted to a larger piston of area A₂: F₂ = PA₂ = F₁A₂/A₁. The applied force is increased by the factor A₂/A₁, the mechanical advantage of the device.' },
    { id: 'e95', topic: 'Example 9.5', t: 'Example 9.5: syringe pistons of diameters 1.0 cm and 3.0 cm connected by water. (a) 10 N on the smaller gives F₂ = (A₂/A₁)F₁ = 90 N. (b) Water being incompressible, L₁A₁ = L₂A₂; pushing the smaller in by 6.0 cm moves the larger out by L₂ = (A₁/A₂)L₁ ≈ 0.67 cm. Atmospheric pressure is common to both and ignored.' },
    { id: 'e96', topic: 'Example 9.6', t: 'Example 9.6: car lift, small piston radius 5.0 cm, second piston radius 15 cm, car mass 1350 kg (g = 9.8). F₁ = (A₁/A₂)F₂ = (5²/15²)(1350 × 9.8) = 1470 N ≈ 1.5 × 10³ N. Pressure P = F₁/A₁ = 1.5 × 10³/π(5 × 10⁻²)² = 1.9 × 10⁵ Pa — almost double the atmospheric pressure.' },
    { id: 'hb1', topic: 'Hydraulic brakes', t: 'In hydraulic brakes a small force on the pedal moves the master piston; the pressure is transmitted through brake oil to a piston of larger area, giving a large force that expands the brake shoes against the brake lining. The pressure is transmitted equally to all cylinders attached to the four wheels, so braking effort is equal on all wheels.' },
    { id: 'sf1', topic: '9.3 Streamline flow', t: 'The study of fluids in motion is fluid dynamics. Flow is steady if at any given point the velocity of each passing fluid particle remains constant in time (velocity may differ at different points). Each particle follows a smooth path and paths do not cross. The path of a fluid particle under steady flow is a streamline — a curve whose tangent at any point is in the direction of the fluid velocity at that point (Fig 9.7).' },
    { id: 'sf2', topic: 'Streamlines do not cross', t: 'No two streamlines can cross; if they did, a particle could go either way and the flow would not be steady. In steady flow the map of flow is stationary in time.' },
    { id: 'sf3', topic: 'Equation of continuity', t: 'Through cross-sections at P, R, Q bounded by the same streamlines: ρ_PA_Pv_PΔt = ρ_RA_Rv_RΔt = ρ_QA_Qv_QΔt (9.9). For incompressible fluids A_Pv_P = A_Rv_R = A_Qv_Q (9.10) — the equation of continuity, a statement of conservation of mass. Av = constant (9.11) gives the volume flux or flow rate. At narrower portions streamlines are closely spaced and velocity increases.' },
    { id: 'sf4', topic: 'Turbulence', t: 'Steady flow is achieved at low speeds. Beyond a limiting value called critical speed the flow loses steadiness and becomes turbulent — as when a fast stream meets rocks, forming small foamy whirlpool-like regions called \'white water rapids\'. Fig 9.8(a) shows laminar streamlines; Fig 9.8(b) a jet of air striking a flat plate — turbulent flow.' },
    { id: 'be1', topic: '9.4 Bernoulli\'s principle', t: 'For steady flow of an incompressible fluid in a pipe of varying cross-section and height (Fig 9.9), velocity changes by continuity, requiring a force, so pressure differs in different regions. Swiss physicist Daniel Bernoulli developed the relation in 1738. Work done on the fluid W₁ − W₂ = (P₁ − P₂)ΔV, which changes kinetic energy ΔK = ½ρΔV(v₂² − v₁²) and potential energy ΔU = ρgΔV(h₂ − h₁).' },
    { id: 'be2', topic: 'Bernoulli\'s equation', t: 'Dividing by ΔV: P₁ + ½ρv₁² + ρgh₁ = P₂ + ½ρv₂² + ρgh₂ (9.12), or P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant (9.13). In words: as we move along a streamline, the sum of the pressure (P), the kinetic energy per unit volume (ρv²/2) and the potential energy per unit volume (ρgh) remains constant.' },
    { id: 'be3', topic: 'Limitations', t: 'Bernoulli assumes no energy loss by friction; real fluids have viscosity (internal friction) converting kinetic energy to heat. So it ideally applies to non-viscous fluids, and to incompressible fluids (elastic energy not considered). It does not hold for non-steady or turbulent flows. For a fluid at rest (v = 0) it gives P₁ − P₂ = ρg(h₂ − h₁), the same as Eq. 9.6.' },
    { id: 'to1', topic: '9.4.1 Torricelli\'s law', t: 'Efflux means fluid outflow. Torricelli found that the speed of efflux from an open tank equals that of a freely falling body. Tank with a hole at height y₁, liquid surface at y₂ under pressure P (Fig 9.10); A₂ >> A₁ so v₂ ≈ 0. Bernoulli: P_a + ½ρv₁² + ρgy₁ = P + ρgy₂, so v₁ = √[2gh + 2(P − P_a)/ρ] (9.14), h = y₂ − y₁.' },
    { id: 'to2', topic: 'v = √(2gh)', t: 'When P >> P_a and 2gh is negligible, efflux speed is determined by container pressure — as in rocket propulsion. If the tank is open to atmosphere (P = P_a), v₁ = √(2gh) (9.15), the speed of a freely falling body — Torricelli\'s law.' },
    { id: 'dl1', topic: '9.4.2 Dynamic lift; Magnus effect', t: 'Dynamic lift is the force on a body (aeroplane wing, hydrofoil, spinning ball) due to its motion through a fluid. A ball moving without spin (Fig 9.11a) has symmetric streamlines — same air speed above and below, zero pressure difference, no vertical force. A spinning ball drags air (more if rough); air speed relative to the ball is larger above and smaller below (Fig 9.11b), streamlines crowd above, giving a net upward force — dynamic lift due to spinning is the Magnus effect. This explains why a spinning ball in cricket, tennis, baseball or golf deviates from its parabolic path.' },
    { id: 'dl2', topic: 'Aerofoil', t: 'An aerofoil is a solid piece shaped to provide upward dynamic lift when moving horizontally through air; aeroplane wing cross-sections look like it (Fig 9.11c). The orientation of the wing makes streamlines crowd above more than below; flow speed on top is higher, giving an upward force that balances the weight of the plane.' },
    { id: 'e97', topic: 'Example 9.7', t: 'Example 9.7: a Boeing of mass 3.3 × 10⁵ kg, wing area 500 m², speed 960 km/h, air density 1.2 kg m⁻³. ΔP A = mg gives ΔP = 3.3 × 10⁵ × 9.8/500 = 6.5 × 10³ N m⁻². ΔP = (ρ/2)(v₂² − v₁²), so (v₂ − v₁)/v_av = ΔP/(ρv_av²) ≈ 0.08 with v_av = 267 m s⁻¹ — the speed above the wing needs to be only 8% higher than below.' },
    { id: 'vi1', topic: '9.5 Viscosity', t: 'Most fluids offer resistance to motion — internal friction called viscosity, existing when there is relative motion between layers. A liquid between two glass plates (Fig 9.12a): bottom fixed, top moving with velocity v; honey needs a greater force than oil for the same velocity, so honey is more viscous. The layer in contact with each surface moves with that surface; velocities increase uniformly from bottom (zero) to top (v); each layer is pulled forward by the upper layer and backward by the lower — laminar flow, like pages of a book sliding.' },
    { id: 'vi2', topic: 'Flow in a pipe', t: 'For a fluid flowing in a pipe or tube, the velocity of the liquid layer along the axis is maximum and decreases gradually towards the walls where it becomes zero; velocity on a cylindrical surface in the tube is constant (Fig 9.12b).' },
    { id: 'vi3', topic: 'Coefficient of viscosity', t: 'In time Δt the shape ABCD becomes AEFD with shear strain Δx/l, which increases continuously with time. Unlike a solid, stress depends on the strain rate Δx/(lΔt) = v/l. Coefficient of viscosity η = (F/A)/(v/l) = Fl/(vA) (9.16) — ratio of shearing stress to strain rate. SI unit poiseuille (Pl); also N s m⁻² or Pa s. Dimensions [ML⁻¹T⁻¹].' },
    { id: 'vi4', topic: 'Temperature; blood', t: 'Thin liquids (water, alcohol) are less viscous than thick liquids (coal tar, blood, glycerine). Blood is \'thicker\' than water, and the relative viscosity η/η_water of blood remains constant between 0 °C and 37 °C. The viscosity of liquids decreases with temperature, while it increases for gases.' },
    { id: 'e98', topic: 'Example 9.8', t: 'Example 9.8: a metal block of area 0.10 m² pulled by a 0.010 kg hanging mass over a pulley (Fig 9.13) moves at constant 0.085 m s⁻¹ on a liquid film 0.30 mm thick. F = mg = 9.8 × 10⁻² N; stress = F/A; strain rate = v/l = 0.085/0.30 × 10⁻³. η = stress/strain rate = (9.8 × 10⁻² × 0.30 × 10⁻³)/(0.085 × 0.10) = 3.46 × 10⁻³ Pa s.' },
    { id: 't92', topic: 'Table 9.2', t: 'Table 9.2 viscosities (mPl): water 20 °C 1.0, 100 °C 0.3; blood 37 °C 2.7; machine oil 16 °C 113, 38 °C 34; glycerine 20 °C 830; honey 200; air 0 °C 0.017, 40 °C 0.019.' },
    { id: 'sk1', topic: '9.5.1 Stokes\' law', t: 'A body falling through a fluid drags the layer in contact, causing relative motion between layers and a retarding force (falling raindrop, swinging pendulum bob). The viscous force is proportional to velocity, opposite to motion, and depends on η and radius a. Sir George G. Stokes (1819–1903), an English scientist, gave the viscous drag F = 6πηav (9.17) — Stokes\' law.' },
    { id: 'sk2', topic: 'Terminal velocity', t: 'A raindrop accelerates initially; as velocity increases the retarding force increases until viscous force plus buoyant force equals the force due to gravity; then net force and acceleration are zero and it falls with constant terminal velocity v_t: 6πηav_t = (4π/3)a³(ρ − σ)g, so v_t = 2a²(ρ − σ)g/(9η) (9.18), ρ and σ being densities of sphere and fluid. v_t depends on the square of the radius and inversely on viscosity.' },
    { id: 'e99', topic: 'Example 9.9', t: 'Example 9.9: copper ball of radius 2.0 mm falls through oil at 20 °C with terminal velocity 6.5 cm s⁻¹; ρ_oil = 1.5 × 10³, ρ_Cu = 8.9 × 10³ kg m⁻³. η = (2/9)a²g(ρ − σ)/v_t = (2/9) × (2 × 10⁻³)² × 9.8 × 7.4 × 10³/6.5 × 10⁻² = 9.9 × 10⁻¹ kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'st1', topic: '9.6 Surface tension', t: 'Oil and water do not mix; water wets us but not ducks; mercury does not wet glass but water sticks to it; oil rises up a cotton wick; sap and water rise to the top of leaves; hair of a paint brush cling together into a fine tip when taken out of water. These relate to free surfaces of liquids, which possess additional energy — surface tension, concerned only with liquids as gases have no free surfaces.' },
    { id: 'se1', topic: '9.6.1 Surface energy', t: 'A molecule well inside a liquid is attracted by all surrounding molecules (Fig 9.14a), giving negative potential energy; the heat of evaporation is large (about 40 kJ/mol for water). A molecule at the surface has only its lower half surrounded (Fig 9.14b), so its negative potential energy is about half that of a bulk molecule. Surface molecules have extra energy; a liquid tends to have the least surface area; increasing surface area requires energy — roughly half the heat of evaporation per molecule.' },
    { id: 'se2', topic: 'What is a surface', t: 'Since molecules move about, there cannot be a perfectly sharp surface; the density drops rapidly to zero around z = 0 within a distance of a few molecular sizes (Fig 9.14c, balance of attractive A and repulsive R forces).' },
    { id: 'ss1', topic: '9.6.2 Surface tension S', t: 'A horizontal liquid film in a frame with a sliding bar (Fig 9.15). Moving the bar by d increases area; work Fd is stored as surface energy. The film has two surfaces, so extra area 2dl: S(2dl) = Fd (9.19), S = Fd/2dl = F/2l (9.20). S is the magnitude of surface tension — the surface energy per unit area of the liquid interface, also the force per unit length exerted by the fluid on the movable bar.' },
    { id: 'ss2', topic: 'Interface; observations', t: 'More generally surface energy is the energy of the interface between two materials and depends on both (attracting molecules reduce it, repelling increase it). (i) Surface tension is a force per unit length (or surface energy per unit area) acting in the plane of the interface; it is the extra energy of molecules at the interface. (ii) At any line on the interface, equal and opposite forces S per unit length act perpendicular to the line in the plane of the interface; at the end of the interface only the inward force acts.' },
    { id: 't93', topic: 'Table 9.3', t: 'Table 9.3 surface tension (N/m) and heat of vaporisation (kJ/mol): helium −270 °C 0.000239, 0.115; oxygen −183 °C 0.0132, 7.1; ethanol 20 °C 0.0227, 40.6; water 20 °C 0.0727, 44.16; mercury 20 °C 0.4355, 63.2. Surface tension depends on temperature; like viscosity, it usually falls with temperature.' },
    { id: 'ss3', topic: 'Measuring S (Fig 9.16)', t: 'A fluid sticks to a solid if the surface energy between fluid and solid is smaller than the sum of solid-air and fluid-air energies. A flat vertical glass plate on a balance arm, with liquid below; the vessel is raised till the liquid touches the plate and pulls it down; weight W is added till the plate just clears the water. S_la = W/2l = mg/2l (9.21), l being the length of the plate edge.' },
    { id: 'ac1', topic: '9.6.3 Angle of contact', t: 'The angle between the tangent to the liquid surface at the point of contact and the solid surface inside the liquid is the angle of contact θ. It differs for different pairs and determines whether a liquid spreads or forms droplets: water forms droplets on a lotus leaf (Fig 9.17a) but spreads over a clean plastic plate (Fig 9.17b). At the line of contact S_la cos θ + S_sl = S_sa (9.22).' },
    { id: 'ac2', topic: 'Obtuse vs acute', t: 'θ is obtuse if S_sl > S_la (water-leaf): liquid molecules are attracted strongly to themselves and weakly to the solid, it costs much energy to create a liquid-solid surface and the liquid does not wet the solid (water on waxy or oily surface, mercury on any surface). If liquid molecules are strongly attracted to the solid, S_sl is reduced, cos θ increases, θ is acute (water on glass or plastic, kerosene on virtually anything).' },
    { id: 'ac3', topic: 'Wetting and waterproofing', t: 'Soaps, detergents and dyeing substances are wetting agents; they make the angle of contact small so that they penetrate well. Water proofing agents create a large angle of contact between water and fibres.' },
    { id: 'db1', topic: '9.6.4 Drops and bubbles', t: 'Free liquid drops and bubbles are spherical if gravity is neglected (small drops in a spray, soap bubbles), since a liquid-air interface has energy and the sphere has the least area for a given volume. Pressure inside a drop is more than outside: expanding radius by Δr needs extra surface energy 8πrΔr S_la (9.23), balanced by work (P_i − P_o)4πr²Δr (9.24), so P_i − P_o = 2S_la/r (9.25).' },
    { id: 'db2', topic: 'Concave side; bubble', t: 'For a liquid-gas interface the concave side (inside) has higher pressure than the convex side — an air bubble in a liquid has higher pressure inside (Fig 9.18b). A soap bubble (Fig 9.18c) has two interfaces: P_i − P_o = 4S_la/r (9.26). This is why you blow hard, but not too hard, to form a soap bubble — a little extra air pressure is needed inside.' },
    { id: 'cr1', topic: '9.6.5 Capillary rise', t: 'Water rises in a narrow tube in spite of gravity (capilla is Latin for hair). In a capillary of radius a with acute contact angle, the water surface is concave; P_i − P_o = 2S/r = (2S/a) cos θ (9.27) with r = a sec θ. So pressure just at the meniscus is less than atmospheric. Points A and B are at the same pressure: P₀ + hρg = P_i = P_A (9.28); hρg = (2S cos θ)/a (9.29), h is the capillary rise.' },
    { id: 'cr2', topic: 'Capillary rise value', t: 'Capillary rise is due to surface tension and is larger for smaller a — typically a few cm for fine capillaries. For a = 0.05 cm and water (S = 0.073 N m⁻¹), h = 2S/(ρga) = 2 × 0.073/(10³ × 9.8 × 5 × 10⁻⁴) = 2.98 × 10⁻² m = 2.98 cm. If the meniscus is convex, as for mercury (cos θ negative), the liquid is lower in the capillary.' },
    { id: 'e910', topic: 'Example 9.10', t: 'Example 9.10: a capillary of diameter 2.00 mm dipped 8.00 cm below water; hemispherical bubble at its end (r = 1.00 mm), S = 7.30 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹. Only one liquid surface, so excess pressure = 2S/r = 146 Pa. P_o = 1.01 × 10⁵ + 0.08 × 1000 × 9.80 = 1.01784 × 10⁵ Pa; P_i = P_o + 2S/r = 1.02 × 10⁵ Pa (for a liquid bubble in gas the excess would be 4S/r).' },
    { id: 'su1', topic: 'Summary 1–7', t: 'A fluid can flow and has no resistance to change of shape. A liquid is incompressible with a free surface; a gas is compressible. P_av = F/A; 1 atm = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa, 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa, 1 torr = 133 Pa = 0.133 kPa, 1 mm of Hg = 1 torr. Pascal\'s law: pressure is the same at all points at the same height, and a change in pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished. P = P_a + ρgh. vA = constant (mass conservation for incompressible flow).' },
    { id: 'su2', topic: 'Summary 8–11', t: 'Bernoulli: P + ρv²/2 + ρgy = constant along a streamline — conservation of energy for non-viscous steady flow; true only approximately as no fluid has zero viscosity. When shear stress is applied to a fluid, shear strain grows with time; η = shear stress/rate of shear strain. Stokes\' law F = 6πηav. Surface tension is a force per unit length (or surface energy per unit area) in the plane of the interface.' },
    { id: 'tb1', topic: 'Summary table', t: 'Pressure P: [ML⁻¹T⁻²], pascal (Pa), scalar. Density ρ: [ML⁻³], kg m⁻³, scalar. Specific gravity: no dimensions, no unit, ρ_substance/ρ_water, scalar. Coefficient of viscosity η: [ML⁻¹T⁻¹], Pa s or poiseuille (Pl), scalar. Surface tension S: [MT⁻²], N m⁻¹, scalar.' },
    { id: 'pp1', topic: 'Points to ponder 1–4', t: 'Pressure is a scalar; the "force" in force per unit area is the normal component. Pressure exists at all points in a fluid, not only on solid walls. P = P_a + ρgh holds for incompressible fluids (liquids). Gauge pressure P − P_a = P_g; tyre pressure gauges and blood pressure gauges (sphygmomanometer) measure gauge pressure.' },
    { id: 'pp2', topic: 'Points to ponder 5–8', t: 'Streamlines do not intersect in steady flow. Bernoulli\'s principle does not hold with viscous drag; P₂ will be lower than given by Eq. 9.12. As temperature rises, η falls for liquids and rises for gases. Surface tension arises from excess potential energy of surface molecules; it exists at an interface of two substances at least one of which is a fluid — not the property of a single fluid alone.' },
    { id: 'xe1', topic: 'Exercises 9.1–9.4', t: 'Blood pressure is greater at the feet than at the brain (taller column). At 6 km atmospheric pressure is about half since density decreases with height. Angle of contact of mercury with glass is obtuse, water with glass acute; water wets glass, mercury does not. Surface tension is independent of area; detergent water should have small angle of contact; a drop under no external forces is spherical. Surface tension of liquids decreases with temperature; viscosity of gases increases and of liquids decreases with temperature. For solids shearing force ∝ shear strain; for fluids ∝ rate of shear strain. Increase in flow speed at a constriction follows conservation of mass. Blowing over paper keeps it horizontal; closing a tap gives fast jets; needle size controls flow rate; efflux gives backward thrust; a spinning ball deviates.' },
    { id: 'xe2', topic: 'Exercises 9.5–9.20', t: '50 kg girl on a 1.0 cm heel: 6.2 × 10⁶ Pa. Wine barometer (ρ = 984): ≈ 10.5 m. Offshore structure at 3 km (3 × 10⁷ Pa) is suitable for 10⁹ Pa. Hydraulic lift 3000 kg, 425 cm²: 6.92 × 10⁵ Pa. Spirit with 10.0 cm water vs 12.5 cm: specific gravity 0.8. Wind tunnel 70 and 63 m s⁻¹, 2.5 m², ρ = 1.3: lift ≈ 1.5 × 10³ N. Spray pump 8.0 cm², 40 holes of 1.0 mm, 1.5 m min⁻¹: 0.64 m s⁻¹. Soap film slider 30 cm, 1.5 × 10⁻² N: S = 2.5 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹. Mercury drop radius 3.00 mm: excess 310 Pa. Soap bubble 5.00 mm, S = 2.5 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹: excess 20 Pa.' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Liquids and gases are called fluids because they:', o: ['Have fixed shape', 'Can flow', 'Have fixed volume', 'Are incompressible'], a: 1, e: '9.1.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Fluids offer very little resistance to shear stress.', 'The shearing stress of fluids is about a million times larger than that of solids.'], a: 2, e: 'It is a million times smaller.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of the following fills the entire volume of its container?', o: ['A liquid', 'A solid', 'A gas', 'Both liquid and solid'], a: 2, e: '9.1.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['pr1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 1, ar: { A: 'A blunt spoon pierces the skin more easily than a sharp needle with the same force.', R: 'Smaller the area on which a force acts, greater the pressure.' }, a: 3, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['pr2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The force exerted by a fluid at rest on a submerged surface is normal to it.', R: 'A parallel component would make the fluid flow parallel to the surface.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['pr3'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A force of 100 N acts normally on an area of 0.5 m². The pressure is:', o: ['50 Pa', '200 Pa', '100 Pa', '20 Pa'], a: 1, e: 'P = F/A.', sol: ['P = F/A', '= 100/0.5', '= 200', 'Answer: 200 Pa'] },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['pr3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Pressure is a vector quantity.', 'The SI unit of pressure is named after Blaise Pascal.'], a: 3, e: 'Pressure is a scalar.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['pr4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The relative density of aluminium is 2.7. Its density is:', o: ['2.7 kg m⁻³', '27 kg m⁻³', '2.7 × 10⁻³ kg m⁻³', '2.7 × 10³ kg m⁻³'], a: 3, e: '9.2.' },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['t91'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the fluid with its density at STP (Table 9.1).', match: { h: ['Fluid', 'ρ (kg m⁻³)'], l: ['Mercury', 'Sea water', 'Air', 'Hydrogen'], r: ['9.0 × 10⁻²', '1.29', '1.03 × 10³', '13.6 × 10³'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 1, e: 'Table 9.1.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['t91'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'According to Table 9.1, the density of interstellar space is about:', o: ['10⁻²⁰ kg m⁻³', '1.29 kg m⁻³', '10⁻² kg m⁻³', '0'], a: 0, e: 'Table 9.1.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['e91'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Two femurs, each of cross-section 10 cm², support an upper body of 40 kg (g = 10 m s⁻²). The average pressure is:', o: ['4 × 10⁵ N m⁻²', '2 × 10³ N m⁻²', '2 × 10⁵ N m⁻²', '10⁵ N m⁻²'], a: 2, e: 'Example 9.1.', sol: ['A = 2 × 10 cm² = 20 × 10⁻⁴ m²', 'F = 40 × 10 = 400 N', 'P = F/A = 400/(2 × 10⁻³)', 'Answer: 2 × 10⁵ N m⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['pl1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'In a fluid at rest, pressure at a point is the same in all directions.', R: 'For the prismatic element, F_b/A_b = F_c/A_c = F_a/A_a.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['pl2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In a liquid at rest, pressure is the same at all points:', o: ['On a vertical line', 'In a horizontal plane', 'Everywhere regardless of depth', 'Only at the surface'], a: 1, e: '9.2.1.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['pd1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The pressure difference between two points 2 m apart vertically in water (ρ = 1000 kg m⁻³, g = 10) is:', o: ['2 × 10³ Pa', '2 × 10⁵ Pa', '200 Pa', '2 × 10⁴ Pa'], a: 3, e: 'P₂ − P₁ = ρgh.', sol: ['ΔP = ρgh', '= 1000 × 10 × 2', '= 20000', 'Answer: 2 × 10⁴ Pa'] },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['pd1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The excess of pressure P − P_a at a depth h is called:', o: ['Absolute pressure', 'Atmospheric pressure', 'Gauge pressure', 'Dynamic pressure'], a: 2, e: '9.2.2.' },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['pd2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 140" width="280" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,20 20,120 260,120 260,20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="21" y="50" width="238" height="69" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><line x1="20" y1="50" x2="260" y2="50" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><polyline points="40,10 40,110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="90,10 120,110 150,10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="190,10 180,110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="240,10 235,110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="30" y="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">C</text><text x="120" y="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A</text><text x="212" y="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B</text><text x="140" y="136" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">same level in all vessels</text></svg>', ar: { A: 'Vessels of different shapes connected at the bottom fill to the same level.', R: 'Liquid pressure depends only on the height of the column, not on the shape of the vessel.' }, a: 0, e: 'Hydrostatic paradox.' },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['pd2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 140" width="280" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,20 20,120 260,120 260,20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="21" y="50" width="238" height="69" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><line x1="20" y1="50" x2="260" y2="50" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><polyline points="40,10 40,110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="90,10 120,110 150,10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="190,10 180,110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="240,10 235,110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="30" y="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">C</text><text x="120" y="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A</text><text x="212" y="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B</text><text x="140" y="136" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">same level in all vessels</text></svg>', q: 'The phenomenon shown in the figure is called:', o: ['Pascal\'s law of transmission', 'Capillarity', 'Magnus effect', 'Hydrostatic paradox'], a: 3, e: 'Fig 9.4.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['e92'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The pressure on a swimmer 10 m below a lake surface (P_a = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa, g = 10) is:', o: ['2.01 × 10⁵ Pa', '1.01 × 10⁵ Pa', '10⁵ Pa', '3.01 × 10⁵ Pa'], a: 0, e: 'P = P_a + ρgh.', sol: ['ρgh = 1000 × 10 × 10 = 10⁵ Pa', 'P = P_a + ρgh', '= 1.01 × 10⁵ + 1.00 × 10⁵', 'Answer: 2.01 × 10⁵ Pa ≈ 2 atm'] },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['at1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 220 200" width="220" height="200" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="80" y="10" width="20" height="160" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="81" y="40" width="18" height="129" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.5"/><polyline points="30,150 30,190 170,190 170,150" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="31" y="160" width="138" height="29" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.5"/><line x1="30" y1="160" x2="170" y2="160" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="106" y="30" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P = 0 (vacuum)</text><line x1="150" y1="120" x2="150" y2="158" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,158 147,151.7 153,151.7" fill="currentColor"/><text x="154" y="116" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Pₐ</text><line x1="70" y1="40" x2="70" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,160 67.8,155.5 72.2,155.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="70" y1="160" x2="70" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,40 72.2,44.5 67.8,44.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="62" y="100" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">h</text><text x="110" y="80" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">mercury</text></svg>', q: 'The mercury barometer was devised by:', o: ['Evangelista Torricelli', 'Blaise Pascal', 'Daniel Bernoulli', 'George Stokes'], a: 0, e: 'Fig 9.5(a).' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['at1', 'at2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 220 200" width="220" height="200" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="80" y="10" width="20" height="160" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="81" y="40" width="18" height="129" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.5"/><polyline points="30,150 30,190 170,190 170,150" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="31" y="160" width="138" height="29" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.5"/><line x1="30" y1="160" x2="170" y2="160" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="106" y="30" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P = 0 (vacuum)</text><line x1="150" y1="120" x2="150" y2="158" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,158 147,151.7 153,151.7" fill="currentColor"/><text x="154" y="116" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Pₐ</text><line x1="70" y1="40" x2="70" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,160 67.8,155.5 72.2,155.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="70" y1="160" x2="70" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,40 72.2,44.5 67.8,44.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="62" y="100" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">h</text><text x="110" y="80" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">mercury</text></svg>', q: 'In a mercury barometer (ρ = 13.6 × 10³ kg m⁻³, g = 9.8), the height h is 76 cm. Atmospheric pressure is about:', o: ['1.01 × 10⁴ Pa', '1.01 × 10⁵ Pa', '7.6 × 10⁵ Pa', '13.6 × 10⁵ Pa'], a: 1, e: 'P_a = ρgh.', sol: ['P_a = ρgh', '= 13.6 × 10³ × 9.8 × 0.76', '= 1.013 × 10⁵', 'Answer: ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa'] },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['at2', 'su1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the unit with its value in pascal.', match: { h: ['Unit', 'Value'], l: ['1 atm', '1 bar', '1 torr', '1 millibar'], r: ['100 Pa', '133 Pa', '10⁵ Pa', '1.01 × 10⁵ Pa'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 2, e: 'Summary 4.' },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['at3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['An open tube manometer measures gauge pressure.', 'For measuring large pressure differences, a low density liquid like oil is used.'], a: 2, e: 'Mercury is used for large differences.' },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['e93'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'If air density (1.29 kg m⁻³) did not change with altitude, the atmosphere (P = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa, g = 9.8) would extend up to about:', o: ['80 km', '0.8 km', '8 km', '100 km'], a: 2, e: 'ρgh = P.', sol: ['h = P/(ρg)', '= 1.01 × 10⁵/(1.29 × 9.8)', '= 7989 m', 'Answer: ≈ 8 km'] },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['e93'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'A drop in the barometer mercury level by 10 mm or more is a sign of:', o: ['Fair weather', 'An approaching storm', 'Earthquake', 'Rising temperature'], a: 1, e: 'Example 9.3.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['e94'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'At a depth of 1000 m in sea water (ρ = 1.03 × 10³ kg m⁻³, g = 10), the gauge pressure is:', o: ['1.04 × 10⁷ Pa', '1.03 × 10⁵ Pa', '1.01 × 10⁵ Pa', '1.03 × 10⁷ Pa'], a: 3, e: 'P_g = ρgh.', sol: ['P_g = ρgh', '= 1.03 × 10³ × 10 × 1000', '= 1.03 × 10⁷', 'Answer: 1.03 × 10⁷ Pa ≈ 103 atm'] },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['e94'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A submarine window of 20 cm × 20 cm at 1000 m depth has gauge pressure 1.03 × 10⁷ Pa on it. The force on it is:', o: ['4.12 × 10⁵ N', '4.12 × 10³ N', '2.06 × 10⁵ N', '1.03 × 10⁶ N'], a: 0, e: 'F = P_g A.', sol: ['A = 0.2 × 0.2 = 0.04 m²', 'F = P_g × A', '= 1.03 × 10⁷ × 0.04', 'Answer: 4.12 × 10⁵ N'] },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['hm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'External pressure applied on any part of a fluid in a vessel is transmitted undiminished in all directions. This is:', o: ['Bernoulli\'s principle', 'Stokes\' law', 'Pascal\'s law', 'Torricelli\'s law'], a: 2, e: '9.2.4.' },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['hm2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 150" width="260" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,40 20,130 240,130 240,50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="21" y="70" width="218" height="59" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><rect x="20" y="64" width="40" height="6" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="140" y="44" width="100" height="8" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="60" y1="40" x2="60" y2="129" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="140" y1="52" x2="140" y2="129" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="40" y1="20" x2="40" y2="62" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,62 37,55.7 43,55.7" fill="currentColor"/><text x="46" y="22" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F₁</text><line x1="190" y1="110" x2="190" y2="56" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="190,56 193,62.3 187,62.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="196" y="90" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F₂</text><text x="40" y="84" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₁</text><text x="190" y="124" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₂</text><rect x="160" y="24" width="60" height="20" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.6"/><text x="190" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">load</text></svg>', q: 'In a hydraulic lift (figure), A₁ = 10 cm² and A₂ = 500 cm². A force of 100 N on the small piston lifts a load of:', o: ['2 N', '5000 N', '500 N', '100 N'], a: 1, e: 'F₂ = F₁A₂/A₁.', sol: ['F₂ = F₁ × A₂/A₁', '= 100 × 500/10', '= 5000', 'Answer: 5000 N'] },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['hm2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 150" width="260" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,40 20,130 240,130 240,50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="21" y="70" width="218" height="59" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><rect x="20" y="64" width="40" height="6" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="140" y="44" width="100" height="8" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="60" y1="40" x2="60" y2="129" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="140" y1="52" x2="140" y2="129" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="40" y1="20" x2="40" y2="62" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,62 37,55.7 43,55.7" fill="currentColor"/><text x="46" y="22" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F₁</text><line x1="190" y1="110" x2="190" y2="56" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="190,56 193,62.3 187,62.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="196" y="90" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F₂</text><text x="40" y="84" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₁</text><text x="190" y="124" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₂</text><rect x="160" y="24" width="60" height="20" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.6"/><text x="190" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">load</text></svg>', q: 'The mechanical advantage of a hydraulic lift is:', o: ['A₁/A₂', 'A₁A₂', '1', 'A₂/A₁'], a: 3, e: '9.2.4.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['e95'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Syringe pistons of diameters 1.0 cm and 3.0 cm are connected by water. 10 N on the smaller piston gives a force on the larger of:', o: ['30 N', '3.3 N', '90 N', '10 N'], a: 2, e: 'Example 9.5.', sol: ['F₂ = (A₂/A₁)F₁', 'A₂/A₁ = (3/1)² = 9', 'F₂ = 9 × 10', 'Answer: 90 N'] },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['e95'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'In Example 9.5, if the smaller piston (d = 1.0 cm) is pushed in by 6.0 cm, the larger piston (d = 3.0 cm) moves out by:', o: ['18 cm', '2 cm', '54 cm', '0.67 cm'], a: 3, e: 'L₁A₁ = L₂A₂.', sol: ['L₂ = (A₁/A₂)L₁', '= (1/9) × 6.0', '= 0.67', 'Answer: ≈ 0.67 cm'] },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['e96'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'In a car lift, small piston radius 5.0 cm, large piston radius 15 cm, car mass 1350 kg (g = 9.8). The force on the small piston is:', o: ['1470 N', '13230 N', '4410 N', '490 N'], a: 0, e: 'F₁ = (A₁/A₂)F₂.', sol: ['F₂ = 1350 × 9.8 = 13230 N', 'A₁/A₂ = (5/15)² = 1/9', 'F₁ = 13230/9', 'Answer: 1470 N ≈ 1.5 × 10³ N'] },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['hb1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'In hydraulic brakes a small force on the pedal produces a large retarding force on the wheel.', R: 'The brake oil acts on a piston of larger area.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['sf1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The flow of a fluid is said to be steady if at any given point:', o: ['The velocity of each passing particle remains constant in time', 'All particles have the same velocity', 'Pressure is zero', 'Fluid is at rest'], a: 0, e: '9.3.' },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['sf1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'A curve whose tangent at any point is along the fluid velocity at that point, in steady flow, is a:', o: ['Equipotential', 'Streamline', 'Isobar', 'Trajectory of a projectile'], a: 1, e: '9.3.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['sf2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Two streamlines can cross in steady flow.', R: 'At the crossing point a particle would have two possible velocities.' }, a: 3, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['sf3'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 110" width="280" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><path d="M 10 30 L 100 30 Q 140 50 180 30 L 270 30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 10 90 L 100 90 Q 140 70 180 90 L 270 90" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="30" y1="60" x2="70" y2="60" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,60 63.7,63 63.7,57" fill="#D9A520"/><line x1="120" y1="60" x2="160" y2="60" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="3"/><polygon points="160,60 153.7,63 153.7,57" fill="#D9A520"/><line x1="210" y1="60" x2="250" y2="60" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="250,60 243.7,63 243.7,57" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="50" y="76" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₁, v₁</text><text x="140" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₂, v₂</text><text x="140" y="106" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">narrow: faster flow, lower pressure</text></svg>', q: 'Water flows through a pipe; at a cross-section of 4 cm² the speed is 2 m s⁻¹. At a section of 1 cm² the speed is:', o: ['0.5 m s⁻¹', '8 m s⁻¹', '2 m s⁻¹', '4 m s⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.', sol: ['A₁v₁ = A₂v₂', '4 × 2 = 1 × v₂', 'v₂ = 8', 'Answer: 8 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['sf3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['At narrower portions of a pipe, streamlines are farther apart.', 'The equation of continuity is a statement of conservation of mass.'], a: 3, e: 'They are closely spaced.' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['sf4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Beyond a limiting speed called critical speed, flow becomes:', o: ['Streamline', 'Laminar', 'Turbulent', 'Steady'], a: 2, e: '9.3.' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['be1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Bernoulli\'s relation was developed by Daniel Bernoulli in:', o: ['1798', '1653', '1851', '1738'], a: 3, e: '9.4.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['be2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Along a streamline P + ½ρv² + ρgh is constant.', 'ρv²/2 is the potential energy per unit volume.'], a: 2, e: 'It is kinetic energy per unit volume.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['be2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 110" width="280" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><path d="M 10 30 L 100 30 Q 140 50 180 30 L 270 30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 10 90 L 100 90 Q 140 70 180 90 L 270 90" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="30" y1="60" x2="70" y2="60" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,60 63.7,63 63.7,57" fill="#D9A520"/><line x1="120" y1="60" x2="160" y2="60" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="3"/><polygon points="160,60 153.7,63 153.7,57" fill="#D9A520"/><line x1="210" y1="60" x2="250" y2="60" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="250,60 243.7,63 243.7,57" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="50" y="76" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₁, v₁</text><text x="140" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A₂, v₂</text><text x="140" y="106" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">narrow: faster flow, lower pressure</text></svg>', q: 'Water flows horizontally; at a point speed is 2 m s⁻¹ and pressure 3 × 10⁴ Pa. Where speed is 4 m s⁻¹ the pressure is (ρ = 1000):', o: ['3.6 × 10⁴ Pa', '1.2 × 10⁴ Pa', '2.4 × 10⁴ Pa', '3 × 10⁴ Pa'], a: 2, e: 'P₁ + ½ρv₁² = P₂ + ½ρv₂².', sol: ['P₂ = P₁ + ½ρ(v₁² − v₂²)', '= 3 × 10⁴ + ½ × 1000 × (4 − 16)', '= 3 × 10⁴ − 6000', 'Answer: 2.4 × 10⁴ Pa'] },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['be3'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Bernoulli\'s equation strictly applies to:', s: ['Non-viscous fluids', 'Incompressible fluids', 'Steady flow', 'Turbulent flow'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: 'Not valid for turbulent flow.' },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['be3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For a fluid at rest, Bernoulli\'s equation reduces to P₁ − P₂ = ρg(h₂ − h₁).', 'Viscosity converts kinetic energy of the fluid into heat.'], a: 0, e: '9.4.' },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['to2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 220 200" width="220" height="200" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="30,20 30,180 170,180 170,20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="31" y="40" width="138" height="139" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><line x1="30" y1="40" x2="170" y2="40" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="170" y1="120" x2="190" y2="120" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="170" y1="128" x2="190" y2="128" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="190" y1="124" x2="215" y2="124" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="215,124 208.7,127 208.7,121" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="212" y="116" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><line x1="150" y1="40" x2="150" y2="124" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,124 147.8,119.5 152.2,119.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="150" y1="124" x2="150" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,40 152.2,44.5 147.8,44.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="144" y="84" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">h</text><text x="100" y="34" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P (top)</text><text x="100" y="196" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v = √(2gh) if open</text></svg>', q: 'Water flows out of a small hole 5 m below the surface of an open tank (g = 10). The speed of efflux is:', o: ['5 m s⁻¹', '50 m s⁻¹', '7 m s⁻¹', '10 m s⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'v = √(2gh).', sol: ['v = √(2gh)', '= √(2 × 10 × 5)', '= √100', 'Answer: 10 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['to2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 220 200" width="220" height="200" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="30,20 30,180 170,180 170,20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="31" y="40" width="138" height="139" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><line x1="30" y1="40" x2="170" y2="40" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="170" y1="120" x2="190" y2="120" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="170" y1="128" x2="190" y2="128" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="190" y1="124" x2="215" y2="124" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="215,124 208.7,127 208.7,121" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="212" y="116" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><line x1="150" y1="40" x2="150" y2="124" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,124 147.8,119.5 152.2,119.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="150" y1="124" x2="150" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,40 152.2,44.5 147.8,44.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="144" y="84" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">h</text><text x="100" y="34" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P (top)</text><text x="100" y="196" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v = √(2gh) if open</text></svg>', ar: { A: 'The speed of efflux from an open tank equals the speed of a body falling freely through the same height.', R: 'Bernoulli\'s equation with P = P_a gives v = √(2gh).' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['to1', 'to2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'When the pressure P above the liquid is much greater than atmospheric and 2gh is negligible, the efflux speed is determined by the container pressure. Such a situation occurs in:', o: ['Rocket propulsion', 'A siphon', 'A barometer', 'A hydraulic lift'], a: 0, e: 'Eq. 9.14.' },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['dl1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The dynamic lift on a spinning ball is called:', o: ['Hydrostatic paradox', 'Magnus effect', 'Capillarity', 'Venturi effect'], a: 1, e: '9.4.2.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['dl1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A spinning cricket ball deviates from its parabolic path.', R: 'A spinning ball does not drag any air with it.' }, a: 2, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['dl1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A rough spinning ball drags less air than a smooth one.', 'A ball moving without spin has symmetric streamlines and no vertical force.'], a: 3, e: 'Rough surface drags more air.' },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['dl2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'An aeroplane wing gets an upward dynamic lift.', R: 'Flow speed above the wing is higher than below, so pressure above is lower.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['e97'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A Boeing of mass 3.3 × 10⁵ kg has wing area 500 m² in level flight (g = 9.8). The pressure difference between the lower and upper wing surfaces is:', o: ['6.5 × 10³ N m⁻²', '6.5 × 10⁵ N m⁻²', '660 N m⁻²', '3.2 × 10³ N m⁻²'], a: 0, e: 'ΔP A = mg.', sol: ['mg = 3.3 × 10⁵ × 9.8 = 3.23 × 10⁶ N', 'ΔP = mg/A', '= 3.23 × 10⁶/500', 'Answer: ≈ 6.5 × 10³ N m⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['e97'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'In Example 9.7, the speed of air above the wing needs to be higher than below by only about:', o: ['80%', '0.8%', '8%', '50%'], a: 2, e: '(v₂ − v₁)/v_av ≈ 0.08.' },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['vi1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 150" width="260" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="240" y2="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="20" y1="130" x2="240" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="20" y1="130" x2="26" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="28" y1="130" x2="34" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="36" y1="130" x2="42" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="44" y1="130" x2="50" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="52" y1="130" x2="58" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="60" y1="130" x2="66" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="68" y1="130" x2="74" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="76" y1="130" x2="82" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="84" y1="130" x2="90" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="92" y1="130" x2="98" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="100" y1="130" x2="106" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="108" y1="130" x2="114" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="116" y1="130" x2="122" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="124" y1="130" x2="130" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="132" y1="130" x2="138" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="140" y1="130" x2="146" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="148" y1="130" x2="154" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="156" y1="130" x2="162" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="164" y1="130" x2="170" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="172" y1="130" x2="178" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="180" y1="130" x2="186" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="188" y1="130" x2="194" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="196" y1="130" x2="202" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="204" y1="130" x2="210" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="212" y1="130" x2="218" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="220" y1="130" x2="226" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="228" y1="130" x2="234" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="236" y1="130" x2="242" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="150" y1="12" x2="210" y2="12" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="210,12 203.7,15 203.7,9" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="214" y="14" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><line x1="60" y1="120" x2="62" y2="120" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="62,120 57.5,122.2 57.5,117.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="95" x2="92" y2="95" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="92,95 87.5,97.2 87.5,92.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="70" x2="122" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="122,70 117.5,72.2 117.5,67.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="45" x2="152" y2="45" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="152,45 147.5,47.2 147.5,42.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="20" x2="182" y2="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="182,20 177.5,22.2 177.5,17.8" fill="currentColor"/><text x="20" y="80" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">l</text><text x="130" y="144" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">fixed plate</text></svg>', q: 'The internal friction of a fluid which resists relative motion between its layers is called:', o: ['Surface tension', 'Pressure', 'Buoyancy', 'Viscosity'], a: 3, e: '9.5.' },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['vi1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 150" width="260" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="240" y2="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="20" y1="130" x2="240" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="20" y1="130" x2="26" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="28" y1="130" x2="34" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="36" y1="130" x2="42" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="44" y1="130" x2="50" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="52" y1="130" x2="58" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="60" y1="130" x2="66" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="68" y1="130" x2="74" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="76" y1="130" x2="82" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="84" y1="130" x2="90" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="92" y1="130" x2="98" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="100" y1="130" x2="106" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="108" y1="130" x2="114" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="116" y1="130" x2="122" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="124" y1="130" x2="130" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="132" y1="130" x2="138" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="140" y1="130" x2="146" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="148" y1="130" x2="154" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="156" y1="130" x2="162" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="164" y1="130" x2="170" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="172" y1="130" x2="178" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="180" y1="130" x2="186" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="188" y1="130" x2="194" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="196" y1="130" x2="202" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="204" y1="130" x2="210" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="212" y1="130" x2="218" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="220" y1="130" x2="226" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="228" y1="130" x2="234" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="236" y1="130" x2="242" y2="136" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="150" y1="12" x2="210" y2="12" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="210,12 203.7,15 203.7,9" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="214" y="14" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><line x1="60" y1="120" x2="62" y2="120" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="62,120 57.5,122.2 57.5,117.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="95" x2="92" y2="95" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="92,95 87.5,97.2 87.5,92.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="70" x2="122" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="122,70 117.5,72.2 117.5,67.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="45" x2="152" y2="45" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="152,45 147.5,47.2 147.5,42.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="60" y1="20" x2="182" y2="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="182,20 177.5,22.2 177.5,17.8" fill="currentColor"/><text x="20" y="80" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">l</text><text x="130" y="144" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">fixed plate</text></svg>', st: ['Honey is more viscous than oil.', 'In laminar flow between plates, the layer in contact with the fixed plate moves fastest.'], a: 2, e: 'It is stationary.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['vi2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For a viscous liquid flowing through a tube, the velocity is maximum:', o: ['Along the axis of the tube', 'At the walls', 'Uniform everywhere', 'Midway between axis and wall'], a: 0, e: 'Fig 9.12(b).' },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['vi3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A plate of area 0.2 m² moves at 0.1 m s⁻¹ over a fixed plate with a 1 mm layer of liquid; force needed is 2 N. The coefficient of viscosity is:', o: ['1 Pa s', '0.1 Pa s', '0.01 Pa s', '10 Pa s'], a: 1, e: 'η = Fl/(vA).', sol: ['η = Fl/(vA)', '= 2 × 10⁻³/(0.1 × 0.2)', '= 2 × 10⁻³/0.02', 'Answer: 0.1 Pa s'] },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['vi3', 'tb1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The dimensions of coefficient of viscosity are:', o: ['[MLT⁻¹]', '[ML⁻¹T⁻¹]', '[ML⁻¹T⁻²]', '[MT⁻²]'], a: 1, e: '9.5.' },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['vi4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The viscosity of a gas increases with temperature.', R: 'Temperature rise increases the random motion of gas atoms.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A (Points to ponder 7).' },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['vi4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The relative viscosity of blood changes greatly between 0 °C and 37 °C.', 'Viscosity of liquids decreases with temperature.'], a: 3, e: 'It remains constant.' },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['e98'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A block of area 0.10 m² moves at 0.085 m s⁻¹ on a 0.30 mm liquid film, pulled by a force of 9.8 × 10⁻² N. The viscosity is about:', o: ['3.46 × 10⁻² Pa s', '0.346 Pa s', '3.46 × 10⁻³ Pa s', '3.46 × 10⁻⁴ Pa s'], a: 2, e: 'Example 9.8.', sol: ['Stress = 9.8 × 10⁻²/0.10 = 0.98 N m⁻²', 'Strain rate = 0.085/0.30 × 10⁻³ = 283 s⁻¹', 'η = 0.98/283', 'Answer: ≈ 3.46 × 10⁻³ Pa s'] },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['t92'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the fluid with its viscosity (Table 9.2).', match: { h: ['Fluid', 'Viscosity (mPl)'], l: ['Water at 20 °C', 'Blood at 37 °C', 'Glycerine at 20 °C', 'Air at 0 °C'], r: ['0.017', '830', '2.7', '1.0'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I'], a: 3, e: 'Table 9.2.' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['sk1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A sphere of radius 1 mm moves at 0.1 m s⁻¹ through a liquid of viscosity 1 Pa s. The viscous drag is:', o: ['6π × 10⁻² N', '6π × 10⁻³ N', 'π × 10⁻⁴ N', '6π × 10⁻⁴ N'], a: 3, e: 'F = 6πηav.', sol: ['F = 6πηav', '= 6π × 1 × 10⁻³ × 0.1', '= 6π × 10⁻⁴', 'Answer: 6π × 10⁻⁴ N ≈ 1.9 × 10⁻³ N'] },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['sk1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Stokes\' law of viscous drag on a sphere is:', o: ['F = 6πηa²v', 'F = 4πηav', 'F = 6πηav', 'F = 6πηv/a'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 9.17.' },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['sk2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'If the radius of a falling drop is doubled, its terminal velocity becomes:', o: ['4 times', '2 times', '½ times', '8 times'], a: 0, e: 'v_t ∝ a².', sol: ['v_t = 2a²(ρ − σ)g/9η', 'v_t ∝ a²', 'a → 2a: v_t → 4v_t', 'Answer: 4 times'] },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['sk2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A raindrop eventually falls with constant velocity.', R: 'Viscous force plus buoyant force becomes equal to its weight.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['e99'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A copper ball (radius 2.0 mm, ρ = 8.9 × 10³) falls through oil (σ = 1.5 × 10³ kg m⁻³) with terminal velocity 6.5 cm s⁻¹ (g = 9.8). Viscosity of oil is about:', o: ['9.9 Pa s', '0.99 Pa s', '0.099 Pa s', '99 Pa s'], a: 1, e: 'Example 9.9.', sol: ['η = (2/9)a²g(ρ − σ)/v_t', '= (2/9) × 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 9.8 × 7.4 × 10³/6.5 × 10⁻²', '= 0.2222 × 0.29/0.065 ≈ 0.99', 'Answer: ≈ 9.9 × 10⁻¹ Pa s'] },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['st1'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 1, q: 'Which phenomena are related to surface tension?', s: ['Oil rising up a cotton wick', 'Hair of a paint brush forming a fine tip', 'Mercury not wetting glass', 'Hydraulic lift'], o: ['I and II only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II and III only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 2, e: 'Hydraulic lift uses Pascal\'s law.' },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['se1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A liquid tends to have the least surface area.', R: 'Molecules at the surface have less energy than those in the interior.' }, a: 2, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['se1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A surface molecule has roughly half the negative potential energy of a bulk molecule.', 'The heat of evaporation of water is about 4 kJ/mol.'], a: 2, e: 'About 40 kJ/mol.' },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['se2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Across the surface of a liquid, its density drops to zero within a distance of the order of:', o: ['A few centimetres', 'A few millimetres', 'One metre', 'A few molecular sizes'], a: 3, e: 'Fig 9.14(c).' },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['ss1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 124" width="240" height="124" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,20 160,20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="20,100 160,100" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="20" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="21" y="21" width="99" height="78" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.35"/><line x1="120" y1="12" x2="120" y2="108" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="120" y1="60" x2="180" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="180,60 173.7,63 173.7,57" fill="currentColor"/><text x="186" y="64" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F</text><line x1="120" y1="75" x2="80" y2="75" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="80,75 86.3,72 86.3,78" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="70" y="114" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">S acts inward on bar</text><line x1="10" y1="20" x2="10" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="10,100 7.8,95.5 12.2,95.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="6" y="64" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">l</text></svg>', q: 'A soap film on a frame with a sliding bar of length 30 cm supports a weight of 1.5 × 10⁻² N (figure). The surface tension is:', o: ['5 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹', '1.25 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹', '2.5 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹', '0.05 N m⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Film has two surfaces: S = F/2l.', sol: ['S = F/2l', '= 1.5 × 10⁻²/(2 × 0.30)', '= 1.5 × 10⁻²/0.6', 'Answer: 2.5 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['ss1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 124" width="240" height="124" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,20 160,20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="20,100 160,100" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="20" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="21" y="21" width="99" height="78" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.35"/><line x1="120" y1="12" x2="120" y2="108" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="120" y1="60" x2="180" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="180,60 173.7,63 173.7,57" fill="currentColor"/><text x="186" y="64" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F</text><line x1="120" y1="75" x2="80" y2="75" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="80,75 86.3,72 86.3,78" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="70" y="114" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">S acts inward on bar</text><line x1="10" y1="20" x2="10" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="10,100 7.8,95.5 12.2,95.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="6" y="64" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">l</text></svg>', ar: { A: 'For a film, S = F/2l rather than F/l.', R: 'A film has two surfaces.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['ss2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Surface energy depends only on the liquid, not on the other material at the interface.', 'Surface tension acts in the plane of the interface.'], a: 3, e: 'It depends on both materials.' },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['ss1', 'pp2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The work done in increasing the area of a soap film (two surfaces) by 10 cm² (S = 0.03 N m⁻¹) is:', o: ['3 × 10⁻⁵ J', '3 × 10⁻² J', '6 × 10⁻³ J', '6 × 10⁻⁵ J'], a: 3, e: 'W = S × 2ΔA.', sol: ['ΔA = 10 cm² = 10⁻³ m² per surface', 'W = S × 2ΔA', '= 0.03 × 2 × 10⁻³', 'Answer: 6 × 10⁻⁵ J'] },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['t93'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Among the liquids in Table 9.3, which has the highest surface tension?', o: ['Mercury', 'Water', 'Ethanol', 'Oxygen'], a: 0, e: '0.4355 N/m.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['t93'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Surface tension of a liquid usually falls with temperature.', 'Water has a surface tension of 0.0727 N/m at 20 °C.'], a: 0, e: 'Table 9.3.' },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['ss3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'In the plate method, an extra mass of 2 g is needed to detach a plate with edge length 10 cm from water (g = 9.8). S_la is about:', o: ['0.098 N m⁻¹', '0.196 N m⁻¹', '0.049 N m⁻¹', '0.98 N m⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'S = mg/2l.', sol: ['S = mg/2l', '= 2 × 10⁻³ × 9.8/(2 × 0.1)', '= 0.0196/0.2', 'Answer: 0.098 N m⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['ac1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 110" width="280" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="80" x2="130" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 30 80 A 32 32 0 1 1 90 80" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.35"/><text x="70" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">θ > 90°: lotus leaf</text><path d="M 30 79 Q 34 68 42 72" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="150" y1="80" x2="270" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 160 80 Q 210 50 260 80" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.35"/><text x="210" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">θ < 90°: clean plastic</text></svg>', q: 'Water forms droplets on a lotus leaf because the angle of contact is:', o: ['Acute', 'Obtuse', 'Zero', 'Exactly 90°'], a: 1, e: 'Fig 9.17(a).' },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['ac1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'At the line of contact of liquid, solid and air, the surface tensions are related by:', o: ['S_la + S_sl = S_sa cos θ', 'S_sa cos θ + S_sl = S_la', 'S_la cos θ + S_sl = S_sa', 'S_la = S_sl = S_sa'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 9.22.' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['ac2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 110" width="280" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10" y1="80" x2="130" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 30 80 A 32 32 0 1 1 90 80" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.35"/><text x="70" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">θ > 90°: lotus leaf</text><path d="M 30 79 Q 34 68 42 72" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="150" y1="80" x2="270" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 160 80 Q 210 50 260 80" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.35"/><text x="210" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">θ < 90°: clean plastic</text></svg>', ar: { A: 'Mercury does not wet glass.', R: 'Mercury molecules are attracted strongly to themselves and weakly to glass, so θ is obtuse.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['ac2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Kerosene spreads on virtually any surface.', 'The angle of contact is acute when S_sl > S_la.'], a: 2, e: 'Obtuse when S_sl > S_la.' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['ac3'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Detergents make the angle of contact small.', 'Water proofing agents create a large angle of contact between water and fibres.'], a: 0, e: '9.6.3.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['db1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 110" width="280" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><circle cx="50" cy="55" r="35" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.4"/><text x="50" y="59" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">drop</text><text x="50" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔP = 2S/r</text><circle cx="140" cy="55" r="35" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="6"/><circle cx="140" cy="55" r="28" fill="none" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="140" y="59" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">cavity</text><text x="140" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔP = 2S/r</text><circle cx="230" cy="55" r="35" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="230" cy="55" r="31" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="230" y="59" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">bubble</text><text x="230" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔP = 4S/r</text></svg>', q: 'Excess pressure inside a mercury drop of radius 3.00 mm (S = 4.65 × 10⁻¹ N m⁻¹) is:', o: ['155 Pa', '310 Pa', '620 Pa', '31 Pa'], a: 1, e: 'ΔP = 2S/r.', sol: ['ΔP = 2S/r', '= 2 × 0.465/3 × 10⁻³', '= 0.93/0.003', 'Answer: 310 Pa'] },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['db1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Small free liquid drops are cubical.', R: 'For a given volume a sphere has the least surface area and hence least surface energy.' }, a: 3, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['db2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 110" width="280" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><circle cx="50" cy="55" r="35" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.4"/><text x="50" y="59" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">drop</text><text x="50" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔP = 2S/r</text><circle cx="140" cy="55" r="35" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="6"/><circle cx="140" cy="55" r="28" fill="none" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="140" y="59" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">cavity</text><text x="140" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔP = 2S/r</text><circle cx="230" cy="55" r="35" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="230" cy="55" r="31" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="230" y="59" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">bubble</text><text x="230" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔP = 4S/r</text></svg>', q: 'Excess pressure inside a soap bubble of radius 5.00 mm (S = 2.50 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹) is:', o: ['10 Pa', '40 Pa', '20 Pa', '5 Pa'], a: 2, e: 'Two surfaces: 4S/r.', sol: ['ΔP = 4S/r', '= 4 × 2.5 × 10⁻²/5 × 10⁻³', '= 0.1/0.005', 'Answer: 20 Pa'] },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['db2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['An air bubble in a liquid has excess pressure 4S/r.', 'The concave side of a liquid-gas interface has higher pressure.'], a: 3, e: 'An air bubble in a liquid has one surface: 2S/r.' },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['cr2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 170" width="240" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,80 20,160 220,160 220,80" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="21" y="110" width="198" height="49" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><line x1="20" y1="110" x2="100" y2="110" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="140" y1="110" x2="220" y2="110" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="105" y1="20" x2="105" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="135" y1="20" x2="135" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="106" y="50" width="28" height="100" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.5"/><path d="M 105 46 Q 120 56 135 46" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="155" y1="50" x2="155" y2="108" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="155,108 152.8,103.5 157.2,103.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="155" y1="108" x2="155" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="155,50 157.2,54.5 152.8,54.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="160" y="82" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">h</text><text x="120" y="14" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">radius a</text></svg>', q: 'Capillary rise of water (S = 0.073 N m⁻¹, θ ≈ 0) in a tube of radius 0.05 cm (g = 9.8) is about:', o: ['29.8 cm', '0.298 cm', '1.49 cm', '2.98 cm'], a: 3, e: 'h = 2S/(ρga).', sol: ['h = 2S cos θ/(ρga)', '= 2 × 0.073/(10³ × 9.8 × 5 × 10⁻⁴)', '= 0.146/4.9', 'Answer: ≈ 2.98 cm'] },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['cr1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 170" width="240" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><polyline points="20,80 20,160 220,160 220,80" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><rect x="21" y="110" width="198" height="49" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.3"/><line x1="20" y1="110" x2="100" y2="110" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="140" y1="110" x2="220" y2="110" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="105" y1="20" x2="105" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="135" y1="20" x2="135" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="106" y="50" width="28" height="100" fill="#D9A520" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.5"/><path d="M 105 46 Q 120 56 135 46" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="155" y1="50" x2="155" y2="108" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="155,108 152.8,103.5 157.2,103.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="155" y1="108" x2="155" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="155,50 157.2,54.5 152.8,54.5" fill="currentColor"/><text x="160" y="82" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">h</text><text x="120" y="14" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">radius a</text></svg>', ar: { A: 'Water rises in a narrow glass capillary.', R: 'Pressure just below the concave meniscus is less than atmospheric pressure.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['cr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Mercury in a glass capillary:', o: ['Rises above the outside level', 'Stays at the same level', 'Spreads on the walls', 'Is depressed below the outside level'], a: 3, e: 'Convex meniscus, cos θ negative.' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['cr1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'If the radius of a capillary is halved, the capillary rise becomes:', o: ['Double', 'Half', 'Four times', 'Same'], a: 0, e: 'h ∝ 1/a.', sol: ['h = 2S cos θ/(ρga)', 'h ∝ 1/a', 'a → a/2: h → 2h', 'Answer: Double'] },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['e910'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A capillary of diameter 2.00 mm blows a hemispherical air bubble in water (S = 7.30 × 10⁻² N m⁻¹). The excess pressure in the bubble is:', o: ['292 Pa', '146 Pa', '73 Pa', '14.6 Pa'], a: 1, e: 'One surface: 2S/r.', sol: ['r = 1.00 mm = 10⁻³ m', 'Excess pressure = 2S/r', '= 2 × 7.30 × 10⁻²/10⁻³', 'Answer: 146 Pa'] },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['e910'], type: 'st', lvl: 3, st: ['In Example 9.10, the bubble radius is taken equal to the capillary radius since it is hemispherical.', 'A bubble of gas in a liquid has two liquid surfaces.'], a: 2, e: 'Only one surface.' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['su1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Pascal\'s law states that a change in pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished.', 'For incompressible flow in a pipe, vA = constant.'], a: 0, e: 'Summary 5, 7.' },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['su2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The ratio of shear stress to the time rate of shearing strain is:', o: ['Coefficient of viscosity', 'Shear modulus', 'Surface tension', 'Bulk modulus'], a: 0, e: 'Summary 9.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['tb1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the quantity with its SI unit.', match: { h: ['Quantity', 'Unit'], l: ['Pressure', 'Density', 'Coefficient of viscosity', 'Surface tension'], r: ['N m⁻¹', 'Pa s', 'kg m⁻³', 'Pa'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 0, e: 'Summary table.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['tb1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Specific gravity has no dimensions and no unit.', 'Surface tension has dimensions [MT⁻²].'], a: 0, e: 'Summary table.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['pp1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A sphygmomanometer measures gauge pressure.', R: 'Gauge pressure is the difference between actual pressure and atmospheric pressure.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R defines gauge pressure.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['pp1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['P = P_a + ρgh holds strictly for gases.', 'Pressure exists at all points in a fluid, not only on the walls.'], a: 3, e: 'For incompressible fluids (liquids).' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['pp2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['With viscous drag, P₂ will be lower than the value given by Bernoulli\'s equation.', 'Surface tension is a property of a single fluid alone.'], a: 2, e: 'It exists at an interface of two substances.' },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['xe1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The blood pressure in humans is greater at the feet than at the brain.', R: 'The height of the blood column above the feet is greater.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['xe1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For fluids, shearing force is proportional to shear strain.', 'For solids, shearing force is proportional to shear strain.'], a: 3, e: 'For fluids it is proportional to rate of shear strain.' },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'To keep a piece of paper horizontal you should blow:', o: ['Under it', 'Over it', 'Along its edge', 'Perpendicular to it from below'], a: 1, e: 'Faster air above lowers pressure.' },
    { id: 'q104', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 50 kg girl balances on a circular heel of diameter 1.0 cm (g = 9.8). The pressure on the floor is about:', o: ['6.2 × 10⁴ Pa', '1.56 × 10⁶ Pa', '6.2 × 10⁶ Pa', '4.9 × 10⁶ Pa'], a: 2, e: 'P = mg/πr².', sol: ['A = π(0.5 × 10⁻²)² = 7.85 × 10⁻⁵ m²', 'F = 50 × 9.8 = 490 N', 'P = 490/7.85 × 10⁻⁵', 'Answer: ≈ 6.2 × 10⁶ Pa'] },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Pascal duplicated Torricelli\'s barometer using wine of density 984 kg m⁻³. The height of the wine column for normal atmospheric pressure (1.01 × 10⁵ Pa, g = 9.8) is about:', o: ['0.76 m', '1.05 m', '105 m', '10.5 m'], a: 3, e: 'h = P/(ρg).', sol: ['h = P/(ρg)', '= 1.01 × 10⁵/(984 × 9.8)', '= 1.01 × 10⁵/9643', 'Answer: ≈ 10.5 m'] },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'In a wind tunnel, flow speeds above and below a model wing are 70 and 63 m s⁻¹; wing area 2.5 m², air density 1.3 kg m⁻³. The lift is about:', o: ['1.5 × 10³ N', '1.5 × 10² N', '6.0 × 10² N', '3.0 × 10³ N'], a: 0, e: 'ΔP = ½ρ(v₂² − v₁²).', sol: ['v₂² − v₁² = 4900 − 3969 = 931', 'ΔP = ½ × 1.3 × 931 ≈ 605 Pa', 'Lift = ΔP × A = 605 × 2.5', 'Answer: ≈ 1.5 × 10³ N'] },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A spray pump tube of cross-section 8.0 cm² has 40 holes of diameter 1.0 mm. If liquid flows in the tube at 1.5 m min⁻¹, the speed of ejection is:', o: ['6.4 m s⁻¹', '0.64 m s⁻¹', '0.025 m s⁻¹', '64 m s⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.', sol: ['A₁ = 8 × 10⁻⁴ m², v₁ = 1.5/60 = 0.025 m s⁻¹', 'A₂ = 40 × π(0.5 × 10⁻³)² = 3.14 × 10⁻⁵ m²', 'v₂ = A₁v₁/A₂ = 2 × 10⁻⁵/3.14 × 10⁻⁵', 'Answer: ≈ 0.64 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A U-tube has 10.0 cm of water in one arm balancing 12.5 cm of spirit in the other. The specific gravity of spirit is:', o: ['1.25', '0.5', '0.8', '1.0'], a: 2, e: 'ρ₁h₁ = ρ₂h₂.', sol: ['ρ_w × 10.0 = ρ_s × 12.5', 'ρ_s/ρ_w = 10/12.5', '= 0.8', 'Answer: 0.8'] },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A hydraulic lift lifts cars up to 3000 kg; load piston area is 425 cm² (g = 9.8). The maximum pressure on the smaller piston is:', o: ['6.92 × 10³ Pa', '7.06 × 10⁴ Pa', '2.94 × 10⁴ Pa', '6.92 × 10⁵ Pa'], a: 3, e: 'Same pressure throughout.', sol: ['F = 3000 × 9.8 = 29400 N', 'A = 425 × 10⁻⁴ m²', 'P = 29400/0.0425', 'Answer: ≈ 6.92 × 10⁵ Pa'] },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['in1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Two-thirds of the earth\'s surface is covered with water.', 'Every mammalian body is mostly water.'], a: 0, e: '9.1.' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['pr1', 'pr2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A circus performer is saved by a wooden plank because the force is spread over a large area.', 'A fluid at rest exerts a force parallel to the surface of a submerged body.'], a: 2, e: 'The force is normal to the surface.' },
    { id: 'q112', p: ['pr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The idealised pressure-measuring device of Fig 9.1(b) consists of:', o: ['A mercury column', 'A U-tube with oil', 'An evacuated chamber with a spring-calibrated piston', 'A glass plate on a balance'], a: 2, e: 'Fig 9.1(b).' },
    { id: 'q113', p: ['pr4'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A liquid of mass 400 g occupies 500 cm³. Its density is:', o: ['800 kg m⁻³', '0.8 kg m⁻³', '1250 kg m⁻³', '80 kg m⁻³'], a: 0, e: 'ρ = m/V.', sol: ['m = 0.4 kg, V = 5 × 10⁻⁴ m³', 'ρ = m/V', '= 0.4/5 × 10⁻⁴', 'Answer: 800 kg m⁻³'] },
    { id: 'q114', p: ['e91'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The total area of the two femurs is 10 cm².', 'In Example 9.1, the force on the femurs is the weight of the upper body.'], a: 3, e: 'Total is 20 cm².' },
    { id: 'q115', p: ['pl1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In the proof of Pascal\'s law (Fig 9.2), the effect of gravity on the prism element is ignored because:', o: ['Gravity is absent in fluids', 'The fluid is moving', 'The element is very large', 'The element is very small'], a: 3, e: '9.2.1.' },
    { id: 'q116', p: ['pl2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['If pressure were unequal at two points in a horizontal plane of a liquid, there would be a flow.', 'Pressure in a liquid at rest increases along a horizontal line.'], a: 2, e: 'It is the same in a horizontal plane.' },
    { id: 'q117', p: ['e92'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['At a depth of 1 km the increase in pressure is about 10 atm.', 'At 10 m depth in a lake, pressure is about 2 atm.'], a: 3, e: 'About 100 atm.' },
    { id: 'q118', p: ['at3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For measuring small pressure differences, an open tube manometer uses:', o: ['A low density liquid such as oil', 'Mercury', 'Air', 'Glycerine only'], a: 0, e: '9.2.3.' },
    { id: 'q119', p: ['hm1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Pushing the piston in Fig 9.6(a) raises fluid level equally in all vertical tubes.', 'Pascal\'s law of transmission does not apply to liquids in closed vessels.'], a: 2, e: 'It applies.' },
    { id: 'q120', p: ['e96'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'In Example 9.6, the force on the small piston (radius 5.0 cm) is 1.5 × 10³ N. The pressure required is about:', o: ['1.9 × 10³ Pa', '1.9 × 10⁵ Pa', '6 × 10⁴ Pa', '1.9 × 10⁶ Pa'], a: 1, e: 'P = F/A.', sol: ['A = π(5 × 10⁻²)² = 7.85 × 10⁻³ m²', 'P = F/A', '= 1.5 × 10³/7.85 × 10⁻³', 'Answer: ≈ 1.9 × 10⁵ Pa (almost 2 atm)'] },
    { id: 'q121', p: ['sf4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['White water rapids are an example of steady flow.', 'Laminar flow (Fig 9.8a) has velocities at different points with parallel directions.'], a: 3, e: 'They are turbulent.' },
    { id: 'q122', p: ['be1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Bernoulli\'s derivation, part of the work done goes into kinetic energy and part into potential energy.', 'Bernoulli\'s equation is derived from conservation of momentum.'], a: 2, e: 'It is from conservation of energy.' },
    { id: 'q123', p: ['to1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Torricelli\'s law derivation, the tank area A₂ is much larger than the hole area A₁.', 'With A₂ >> A₁, the liquid at the top is taken to be nearly at rest.'], a: 0, e: '9.4.1.' },
    { id: 'q124', p: ['dl2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Streamlines crowd below the wing more than above it.', 'An aerofoil is shaped to provide upward dynamic lift.'], a: 3, e: 'They crowd above the wing.' },
    { id: 'q125', p: ['vi2'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['In a pipe, the velocity of a viscous liquid becomes zero at the walls.', 'The velocity on a cylindrical surface in the tube is constant.'], a: 0, e: 'Fig 9.12(b).' },
    { id: 'q126', p: ['e98'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Example 9.8, the shear force on the liquid equals the weight of the hanging mass.', 'The strain rate is v/l.'], a: 0, e: 'Example 9.8.' },
    { id: 'q127', p: ['t92'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'From Table 9.2, which is more viscous?', o: ['Water at 20 °C', 'Blood at 37 °C', 'Water at 100 °C', 'Air at 40 °C'], a: 1, e: '2.7 mPl.' },
    { id: 'q128', p: ['e99'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Example 9.9, the density of copper is 8.9 × 10³ kg m⁻³.', 'Terminal velocity is independent of the density of the oil.'], a: 2, e: 'It depends on (ρ − σ).' },
    { id: 'q129', p: ['st1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Water wets ducks.', 'Surface tension is concerned only with liquids since gases do not have free surfaces.'], a: 3, e: 'Water wets us but not ducks.' },
    { id: 'q130', p: ['se2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A liquid surface is not perfectly sharp.', 'At the surface, attractive and repulsive forces balance (Fig 9.14c).'], a: 0, e: '9.6.1.' },
    { id: 'q131', p: ['ss2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'If a line is imagined on a liquid surface, surface tension forces act on it:', o: ['Along the line', 'Normal to the surface', 'Perpendicular to the line, in the plane of the interface', 'Only downward'], a: 2, e: '9.6.2 (ii).' },
    { id: 'q132', p: ['ss3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A fluid sticks to a solid if the fluid-solid surface energy is smaller than the sum of solid-air and fluid-air energies.', 'In the plate method, S_la = W/l.'], a: 2, e: 'S_la = W/2l.' },
    { id: 'q133', p: ['ac3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Soaps and detergents act as wetting agents because they:', o: ['Make the angle of contact large', 'Increase surface tension', 'Increase viscosity', 'Make the angle of contact small'], a: 3, e: '9.6.3.' },
    { id: 'q134', p: ['su2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'According to Stokes\' law, the viscous drag on a sphere is proportional to:', o: ['Its radius and velocity', 'Square of radius', 'Square of velocity', 'Its density'], a: 0, e: 'F = 6πηav.' },
    { id: 'q135', p: ['hb1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The braking effort is larger on the front wheels than on the rear wheels.', 'In hydraulic brakes, the master cylinder piston transmits pressure through brake oil.'], a: 3, e: 'It is equal on all wheels.' },
    { id: 'q136', p: ['sf2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In steady flow, the map of streamlines is:', o: ['Changing continuously', 'Stationary in time', 'Random', 'Circular always'], a: 1, e: '9.3.' }
  ]
});
